      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "a)\nTo solve the problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the sale price after the initial 20% discount.**\n2. **Calculate the final price after applying the 25% coupon discount to the sale price.**\n3. **Determine the single percent discount that would result in the same final price.**\n\nLet's start with the first step:\n\n1. **Calculate the sale price after the initial 20% discount:**\n   - The original price of the shirt is $30.\n   - A 20% discount means the shirt is sold at 80% of its original price.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Sale price} = 30 \\times (1 - 0.20) = 30 \\times 0.80 = 24\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the final price after applying the 25% coupon discount to the sale price:**\n   - The sale price after the 20% discount is $24.\n   - A 25% coupon discount means the shirt is sold at 75% of the sale price.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Final price} = 24 \\times (1 - 0.25) = 24 \\times 0.75 = 18\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the single percent discount that would result in the same final price:**\n   - We need to find a single discount \\(D\\) such that \\(30 \\times (1 - D) = 18\\).\n   - Solving for \\(D\\):\n   \\[\n   1 - D = \\frac{18}{30} = 0.60\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   D = 1 - 0.60 = 0.40\n   \\]\n   - Converting this to a percentage:\n   \\[\n   D = 0.40 \\times 100\\% = 40\\%\n   \\]\n\nThus, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
