      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let's start by calculating the sale price of the shirt after the initial 20% discount.\n\nThe regular price of the shirt is $30. With a 20% discount, the sale price is:\n\\[\n30 \\times (1 - 0.20) = 30 \\times 0.80 = 24\n\\]\nSo, the sale price of the shirt is $24.\n\nNext, Mary applies a 25% coupon to the sale price of $24. The remaining 25% discount off $24 is:\n\\[\n24 \\times (1 - 0.25) = 24 \\times 0.75 = 18\n\\]\nTherefore, the final price after applying the 25% coupon to the sale price of $24 is $18.\n\nTo find the single percent discount that would give the same final price, we need to determine what percent reduction from the initial regular price ($30) results in $18. Let \\( x \\) be the single percent discount. The final price is:\n\\[\n30 \\times (1 - \\frac{x}{100}) = 18\n\\]\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n1 - \\frac{x}{100} = \\frac{18}{30} = 0.6\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{100} = 1 - 0.6 = 0.4\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 0.4 \\times 100 = 40\n\\]\nTherefore, the single percent discount that would give the same final price is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
