      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the final price after the successive discounts and find the equivalent single discount, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the sale price after the first discount of 20%.\n2. Calculate the price after the additional 25% discount based on the new sale price.\n3. Determine the single equivalent discount rate to get the same final price.\n\nLet's start with the first step:\n\n1. **Calculate the sale price after the first discount:**\n   The regular price of the shirt is $30. A 20% discount means the shirt is sold for 80% of its original price.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Sale Price} = 30 \\times (1 - 0.20) = 30 \\times 0.80 = 24\n   \\]\n   So, the sale price after the first discount is $24.\n\n2. **Calculate the price after the additional 25% discount:**\n   The new price is $24. A 25% discount means this new price is 75% of its current price.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Final Price} = 24 \\times (1 - 0.25) = 24 \\times 0.75 = 18\n   \\]\n   So, the final price after both discounts is $18.\n\n3. **Determine the single equivalent discount rate:**\n   The original price was $30. The final price is $18. The difference between the original price and the final price is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Difference} = 30 - 18 = 12\n   \\]\n   The single equivalent discount rate is the fraction of the original price that is saved, multiplied by 100 to get the percentage.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Single Equivalent Discount Rate} = \\left( \\frac{12}{30} \\right) \\times 100 = 40\\%\n   \\]\n\nThus, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
